infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Looser vs tighter — choosing the constraint
  desc Constrain to exactly the members the body touches — looser and you cannot write the body, tighter and callers pay for requirements they do not need
  compares
    - label Looser constraint
      children
        - label Accepts more callers
          desc A broad bound makes more argument types assignable, so fewer are rejected at the call site
        - label Unlocks fewer members
          desc The body cannot touch what is not guaranteed, falling back to casts or runtime checks
        - label Too loose breaks the body
          desc Constrain below what the body reads and you cannot use the value you constrained
        - label Loosening later is silent
          desc Drop a required member and callers still compile, but the body that read it needs an unwritten fallback
    - label Tighter constraint
      children
        - label Unlocks more members
          desc Every member of the bound is guaranteed, so the body may read them freely
        - label Rejects more callers
          desc An argument missing a required member no longer fits — a loud error at the call site
        - label Couples callers to extras
          desc Naming members the body never reads forces requirements callers do not need
        - label Tightening ripples loudly
          desc Adding one required member errors every call site — a PR touching ~40 files
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6
